Figure 1: Charge transfer in guest-MOF systems via hopping processes. (a) Possible first order transfer processes (solid lines) in a MOF-guest system are the subsequent hopping between guest and MOF sites (1) and the direct transfer between guest-guest or MOF-MOF sites (2,4). Processes (1) and (2) are suppressed by large \(\Delta\mathrm{E}\) and low J, respectively, and the rate of process (4) is limited because MOF-states are not occupied. In order to explain high current densities in the MOF-guest system, additional second order-processes (superexchange, dashed lines) are considered (3,5). (b) Transfer between the LUMO orbitals of the guest molecules can proceed directly or via occupancy of a virtual state in the MOF. In the superexchange process the energy difference, \(\Delta\mathrm{E_{gh}}\), enters linearly in the rate, while it enters exponentially in guest-host hopping processes.
Figure 2: Binding mechanism of F4-TCNQ in HKUST-1: each nitrogen atom of the guest molecules is attached to a copper atom of HKUST-1. The total binding energy is \(-1.97\mathrm{eV}\) for TCNQ and \(-1.82\mathrm{eV}\) for F4-TCNQ. This configuration is energetically favorable by \(0.72\mathrm{eV}\) for TCNQ and \(0.66\mathrm{eV}\) for F4-TCNQ in comparison to other configurations. In the preferred configuration, each cavity can host up to two guest molecules, resulting in a large number of additional hopping sites in the MOF.
Figure 3: Marcus transfer rates and electron mobility calculated with kinetic Monte Carlo (KMC) simulations. Marcus transfer rates are calculated for three different transfer processes between guest molecules TCNQ (a) and F4-TCNQ (b) in the MOF: (i) the direct first order charge transfer (blue / cyan) (ii) the first order charge transfer from guest molecule to HKUST-1 and from HKUST-1 onto the subsequent guest molecule (green / black) and (iii) the second order process (superexchange) between guest molecules, where the HKUST-1 intermediate state is only virtually occupied (red / orange). The first order guest-MOF-guest process is predominant for \(\Delta\mathrm{E}<0.4\), whereas the superexchange process is orders of magnitude faster for \(\Delta\mathrm{E}>0.4\) for both TCNQ and F4-TCNQ. (c): Mobility from KMC simulations with superexchange (SX, red (TCNQ) / orange (F4-TCNQ)) are up to 5 orders of magnitude larger than the mobility calculated on the basis of the first order processes (blue (TCNQ), cyan (F4-TCNQ)) for both guest molecules. In order to compare mobilities of both guest molecules, the mobility of F4-TCNQ is shifted by \(0.6\mathrm{eV}\) along the x-axis, as the LUMO-level of F4-TCNQ is estimated to differ by at least \(0.6\mathrm{eV}\) from the LUMO-level of TCNQ. (d) Hole hopping transport in HKUST-1 loaded with TCNQ and F4-TCNQ. First order MOF-guest-MOF hopping rates for both TCNQ (green) and F4-TCNQ (black) exceed the direct MOF-MOF rate (blue) for energy differences \(\Delta\mathrm{E}<0.25\mathrm{eV}\) by only less than 2 orders of magnitude. The second order (superexchange) rates (red: TCNQ, orange: F4-TCNQ) are orders of magnitude below the first order MOF-MOF-rates (e) Hole mobility extracted from KMC simulations show that for \(\Delta\mathrm{E}>0.5\ \mathrm{eV}\) the mobility of the guest-host systems is the same as the mobility in the pristine HKUST-1 (blue). For \(\Delta\mathrm{E}<0.5\mathrm{eV}\), the guest molecules induce a maximal increase of one order of magnitude for both TCNQ (green-solid: no superexchange, red-dashed: including superexchange) and F4-TCNQ (black-solid: no superexchange, yellow-dashed: including superexchange).
Figure 6: Mobility derived from the experimental data using an injection model (dashed lines) and KMC calculations (solid lines) as a function of the energy difference between the LUMO of the pristine vs. the loaded SURMOF. The mobility curves intersect at approx. \(0.62~\mathrm{eV}\) and \(0.71~\mathrm{eV}\) for F4-TCNQ (yellow) and TCNQ (red), respectively.
Table 1: Coupling matrix elements between guest molecules, guest molecule and MOF-orbital and MOF-MOF-orbitals as well as reorganization energies \(\uplambda\) for guest molecules and MOF-orbitals. For the coupling between the MOF-hopping sites, first and second nearest neighbors are considered. Two different alignments of guest molecules in neighboring cavities (orthogonal, o, and parallel, p) can be observed in the model.
